+++ b/article-reels/README.md
@@ -48,3 +48,78 @@ with `npm run gui`.
 Each reel is roughly 1-3 MB. GitHub blocks files over 100 MB and warns once a
 repo passes ~1 GB, so this is fine for years of posts; if it ever matters,
 switch the workflow to artifacts only, or move this folder to Git LFS.
+
+## Scheduling the reels
+
+`schedule-reels.py` uploads the reels in this folder as **scheduled**
+(unpublished) video posts, so Facebook publishes each one at its own time. The
+title and description come from the file name (the article slug).
+
+```bash
+python3 article-reels/schedule-reels.py --dry-run          # preview the plan
+FB_PAGE_TOKEN=... python3 article-reels/schedule-reels.py  # schedule all reels
+FB_PAGE_TOKEN=... python3 article-reels/schedule-reels.py \
+    --start-date 2026-10-20 --time 09:30 --interval-days 2 --limit 5
+```
+
+| Option | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `--folder` | Folder holding the reels (default: this folder) |
+| `--page-id` | Target Page (default: the Sin City page, or `FB_PAGE_ID`) |
+| `--time` / `--interval-days` | Publish time (UTC) and spacing between reels |
+| `--limit` | Schedule only the first N reels |
+| `--dry-run` | Print the plan and post nothing; needs no token |
+| `--force` | Ignore the manifest and re-schedule everything |
+
+It keeps a `.reel-schedule-manifest.json` so re-running never re-posts the same
+file. **All times are UTC**, and Facebook only accepts a scheduled time between
+~10 minutes and ~6 months out.
+
+One caveat: a re-run lays out slots from `--start-date` without consulting the
+times the manifest already holds for *other* reels, so two reels can land on the
+same publish minute.
+
+## Tracking page posts
+
+```bash
+FB_PAGE_TOKEN=... python3 article-reels/scrape-page-posts.py \
+    --page 105407425560140 --count 30
+FB_PAGE_TOKEN=... python3 article-reels/scrape-page-posts.py \
+    --page "Sin City" --count 20 --csv posts.csv
+```
+
+Reads the Graph API `/{page-id}/feed` edge. Pages you manage work with your page
+token; for pages you do not manage the app also needs **Page Public Metadata
+Access** or Meta returns error 10. Writes JSON (`--out`, default
+`page-posts.json`) and optionally a CSV.
+
+## Dashboard
+
+```bash
+python3 article-reels/generate_dashboard.py
+# then open article-reels/dashboard.html
+```
+
+Reads the schedule manifest plus the scraped `page-posts.json` /
+`sumona-posts.json` and writes a self-contained `dashboard.html` - the data is
+embedded, so it works offline. `dashboard.css` and `dashboard.js` are its
+siblings. The generated HTML is not committed; regenerate it for fresh numbers.
+
+It shows scheduled reels with live countdowns, the next publish, and the
+engagement on the tracked pages' recent posts.
+
+## Tokens
+
+None of these scripts hardcode a token - each reads `FB_PAGE_TOKEN` (or takes
+`--token`) and exits with a clear message when it is missing. Mint one with
+`fb-oauth.py`:
+
+```bash
+python3 article-reels/fb-oauth.py auth-url --app-id YOUR_APP_ID
+# open the URL, approve the permissions, copy `code` from the redirect URL
+python3 article-reels/fb-oauth.py exchange --app-id YOUR_APP_ID \
+    --app-secret YOUR_APP_SECRET --code THE_CODE
+```
+
+It prints a long-lived user token (~60 days) and the page access tokens for
+every page you manage.
